matched_variant_raw.rewritten_question_text:
Ammonia (NH$_3$) and nitrous oxide (N$_2$O) undergo the following reaction:

$$2\text{NH}_3(\text{g}) + 3\text{N}_2\text{O}(\text{g}) \rightarrow 4\text{N}_2(\text{g}) + 3\text{H}_2\text{O}(\text{g})$$

The enthalpy values of various bonds are known:
the N-H bond enthalpy is $388\text{kJ/mol}$;
the N-O bond enthalpy is $210\text{kJ/mol}$;
the N=O bond enthalpy is $630\text{kJ/mol}$;
the N≡N bond enthalpy is $941\text{kJ/mol}$;
the O-H bond enthalpy is $463\text{kJ/mol}$.

If 25.00 grams of NH$_3$ and 25.00 grams of N$_2$O react completely, what is the magnitude of the energy change?

